t cystoscopy is tied to tumor detection\",\"explanation\":\"Cystoscopy is commonly performed using white light for visualization. This establishes the default illumination mode in the community’s description of how cystoscopy is typically conducted. [Data: Entities (5794); Relationships (13130)]\\n\\nA more specific entity, white-light cystoscopy, is explicitly connected to detecting tumors inside the bladder. The relationship notes that white light cystoscopy is used to see inside the bladder and detect tumors, though it may show tumors less distinctly than blue-light approaches (as described in the relationship text). Even without the blue-light entity being present, the dataset still distinguishes the standard white-light approach from other possible modalities by emphasizing detection performance context. [Data: Entities (5811); Relationships (13179)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Defined post-procedure side effects are part of the cystoscopy community context\",\"explanation\":\"The network includes an entity describing side effects after cystoscopy and links it directly to the cystoscopy procedure. The side effects described include bleeding from the urethra (e.g., pink urine or blood on toilet paper), burning during urination, and increased frequency of urination for a day or two. [Data: Entities (5801, 4684); Relationships (13144)]\\n\\nThe presence of side effects as a first-class connected entity suggests that patient outcome expectations and procedural aftercare considerations are integral to how cystoscopy is communicated and understood within this community dataset, not just the technical act of performing the procedure. [Data: Entities (5801); Relationships (13144)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Bladder cancer diagnosis is positioned as multi-test confirmation, with cystoscopy within the evaluation pathway\",\"explanation\":\"Bladder cancer diagnosis is described as requiring multiple tests—such as blood and urine tests, cystoscopy/biopsy, and imaging—to confirm disease and a